            <ArticleTitle>An unusual presentation of chest pain: needle perforation of the right ventricle.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>218-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.hrtlng.2013.02.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0147-9563(13)00059-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Foreign bodies in the heart are a rare occurrence and can result from intravenous drug abuse, trauma or iatrogenic causes. There are no current guidelines for the treatment of a cardiac foreign body. We hereby present a brief review of the available literature and report a case of a woman with chest pain subsequently complicated by cardiogenic shock due to tamponade secondary to a needle fragment perforating her right ventricular free wall.</AbstractText>
